<!--
    AML endpoint table.

    AML destinations are run by national emergency services. Most of them
    were agreed privately between the PSAP, the mobile operators and the
    big OS vendors, and are not published anywhere. Sending an emergency
    location to the wrong place helps nobody, so the rule here is simple:

      * A country only gets an <sms> or <https> line when an official or
        primary source says where AML goes. Every entry cites its source.
      * Countries we care about but could not find a public endpoint for
        are listed with their triggers only. They do nothing until someone
        adds an endpoint obtained from the PSAP operator.

    You can replace this table without touching the app:
      * drop a file at /product/etc/cipheraml/aml_endpoints.xml or
        /system_ext/etc/cipheraml/aml_endpoints.xml, or
      * overlay this resource with an RRO.

    Format:

    <country name="..."          Free text, only used in logs and Settings.
             mcc="234,235"       Network MCCs this entry applies to.
             iso="gb"            Country ISO codes, used when the MCC is
                                 not known yet (limited service).
             confidence="90"     Optional, overrides config_amlConfidencePercent.
             roaming-sms="false"> Also send the SMS from foreign SIMs. Off by
                                 default: a foreign SIM sends through its home
                                 network, which would read the destination in
                                 its own numbering plan. Only turn this on when
                                 a source documents a roaming route.

        <trigger number="999" /> Numbers that start AML. Leave out every
        <trigger number="112" /> trigger to start on any emergency number.

        <sms destination="999"   Where the SMS goes.
             type="text"         "text" (default) or "data".
             port="..."          Destination port, only for type="data".
             optional-fields="false"  Also send al= and toc= when they fit.
             partial-imei="false" />  Send only the TAC part of the IMEI.

        <https url="https://..." />  Annex B HTTPS endpoint. https only.
    </country>

    Sources referred to below:
      [ETSI]  ETSI TS 103 625 V1.2.1, Transporting Handset Location to
              PSAPs for Emergency Communications, Advanced Mobile Location.
      [EENA]  EENA, AML Report Card 2023 Update. Per country questionnaire
              answered by the national authorities.
-->
<aml-endpoints version="1">

    <!--
        United Kingdom.

        AML SMS goes to 999 and is picked up by BT, which runs the Stage 1
        PSAP and the AML end-point. SMS to 999 for AML is zero rated.

          [ETSI] clause 6.3.4.1 refers to "the normal 999 code for UK AML
                 messages".
          [EENA] United Kingdom, 3.1: SMS to short number for Android and
                 iOS. 3.3: zero rated. 3.4: BT Stage 1 PSAP.
                 1.6: AML is activated for 112 and 999.

        Jersey, Guernsey and the Isle of Man share MCC 234 but run their
        own emergency services, so AML there may not reach BT.
    -->
    <country name="United Kingdom" mcc="234,235" iso="gb">
        <trigger number="999" />
        <trigger number="112" />
        <sms destination="999" />
    </country>

    <!--
        Slovakia.

        AML SMS goes to 112 and is handled by the Ministry of Interior.
        Zero rated.

          [EENA] Slovakia, 3.1: SMS to short number for Android, iOS and
                 HarmonyOS, additional details "SMS to 112 number".
                 3.3: zero rated. 3.4: Ministry of Interior of the Slovak
                 Republic. 1.6: AML is activated for 112, 150 (fire and
                 rescue), 155 (emergency medical) and 158 (police).
    -->
    <country name="Slovakia" mcc="231" iso="sk">
        <trigger number="112" />
        <trigger number="150" />
        <trigger number="155" />
        <trigger number="158" />
        <sms destination="112" />
    </country>

    <!--
        Lithuania.

        AML SMS goes to 112 and is handled by the Emergency Response
        Centre (Bendrasis pagalbos centras), which runs 112 in Lithuania.

          112.lt, "Locating the caller" (Emergency Response Centre): "When
          calling 112 from a smartphone with an updated Android or iOS
          operating system, [...] the handset itself determines its location
          [...] and automatically sends its coordinates to 112 in a coded
          SMS."
          https://112.lt/en/calling-number-112/locating-the-caller/

        Android also triggers on the old national numbers (101, 011, 102,
        022) according to the EENA 2020 report card, but nothing public says
        where those messages go, so we only trigger on 112.
    -->
    <country name="Lithuania" mcc="246" iso="lt">
        <trigger number="112" />
        <sms destination="112" />
    </country>

    <!--
        Australia.

        Required by the Australian handset standard: a plain text AML v1
        SMS to 1262612626, which the carriers hand to Telstra, the
        Emergency Call Person for 000 and 112. It applies to any compliant
        handset, not just one vendor.

          AS/CA S042.1:2022, clause 5.2.2.1(g)(ii): "Within 25 seconds after
          successful initiation of an Emergency Call, CE that incorporates
          AML functionality shall initiate a Short Message (SM) containing
          AML information to the Australian number 1262612626."
          Clause 5.2.2.1(h): handsets "shall not initiate" it "when the CE
          is roaming outside Australia". Communications Alliance G557.6:2021
          also leaves inbound international roamers out, which the default
          roaming-sms="false" takes care of.
          https://www.commsalliance.com.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/83696/S042-1_2022.pdf

        Clause 5.2.2.1(g)(iv) says the SMS "should" carry IMEI and IMSI. We
        send the full IMEI and cut the IMSI down to MCC and MNC, as
        TS 103 625 annex A asks. 106, the text relay emergency number, is
        not a trigger.
    -->
    <country name="Australia" mcc="505" iso="au">
        <trigger number="000" />
        <trigger number="112" />
        <sms destination="1262612626" />
    </country>

    <!--
        Germany. Endpoint pending, nothing is sent yet.

        What is public:
          * There is one national AML end-point, run by the Integrierte
            Leitstelle Freiburg-Breisgau-Hochschwarzwald, with a passive
            backup at the Berliner Feuerwehr. Over 230 PSAPs pull from it.
          * It takes SMS to a short number (Android and iOS) and HTTPS
            (Android), zero rated. Roamers are covered through HTTPS.
          * SMS and HTTPS copies of the same fix are matched on the IMEI,
            so do not set partial-imei here.
          * Freiburg receives three messages per call, at 0, 15 and 30
            seconds. CipherAML sends one for now.
          * 112 triggers AML everywhere. 110 is being added state by state.

        What is not public: the SMS short code and the HTTPS URL. Both were
        agreed between Freiburg, the operators and the OS vendors. To turn
        Germany on, ask ILS Freiburg for access and add the <sms> and/or
        <https> lines they give you.

          [EENA] Germany, 1.6, 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.6.
          ILS Freiburg, "AML: Matching SMS with HTTPS", EENA webinar, 2022.
          Help112 II D5.1 (2020), table 4-7: roaming via HTTPS.
          teltarif.de, 2024: AML for 110 rolling out per federal state.
    -->
    <country name="Germany" mcc="262" iso="de">
        <trigger number="112" />
        <trigger number="110" />
    </country>

    <!--
        India. Endpoint pending, nothing is sent yet.

        What is public:
          * Google's Android ELS went live with UP 112 (Uttar Pradesh) in
            December 2025, integrated by UP Police with Pert Telecom
            Solutions. Other states are expected to follow under ERSS.
          * It triggers on calls and SMS to 112.

        What is not public: where the location goes. The integration is
        private and no AML number or URL has been published.

        Do NOT add <sms destination="112" /> here as a guess. ERSS takes
        regular SMS to 112 from people (it is one of its official
        channels), so an AML message there would land in front of a call
        taker as a string of gibberish in the middle of an emergency.

        To turn India on, get the end-point from ERSS (Ministry of Home
        Affairs, C-DAC) or the state 112 service and add it below.

          MHA, Emergency Response Support System (ERSS) page: SMS listed
          as a 112 channel.
          Launch coverage of Android ELS in Uttar Pradesh, December 2025.
    -->
    <country name="India" mcc="404,405" iso="in">
        <trigger number="112" />
    </country>
</aml-endpoints>
